{
 "title": "Mathematics and Computation",
 "byline": "Avi Wigderson \u00b7 Princeton University Press, 2019 (author\u2019s online draft, August 2019)",
 "note": "The long reference list is not broken down. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "A panoramic tour of the theory of computation written for mathematicians and curious scientists rather than specialists. Wigderson surveys computational complexity (P, NP, reductions, circuits and lower bounds, proof complexity), the role of randomness (pseudo-randomness, derandomization, expanders, extractors), interactive and probabilistically checkable proofs, quantum and arithmetic complexity, and models of limited resources (space, communication, online, learning, cryptography, distributed computing). A long interlude shows concrete two-way traffic between complexity and fields from number theory to invariant theory, and the epilogue argues that computation is a lens on all of science.",
